WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
Remember the white-eyed fruit fly that hatched in

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:04.160
Morgan's lab in 1910?

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.440
He crossed it with red-eyed females, and almost

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.600
all their offspring had red eyes.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.920
So white was recessive.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.120
Then he bred those offspring with each other,

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.520
and white eyes came back, as expected.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.040
But there was a twist.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.000
Every single white-eyed fly was male.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.440
Why would eye colour care whether a fly

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:26.880
is male or female?

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.700
The answer is in the sex chromosomes.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.100
In fruit flies, as in humans, females have

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:37.140
two X chromosomes, and males have one X

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.500
and one Y.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.060
The eye colour gene is on the X,

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.660
and the Y doesn't carry it.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.060
So a female has two copies of the

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.300
gene, but a male has only one.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.340
If that one copy is the white allele,

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:52.500
there's no other allele to hide it, so

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:54.020
his eyes are white.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.580
A gene on a sex chromosome is called

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:57.780
sex-linked.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.960
Now Morgan's second cross.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.480
The red-eyed daughters each had a red allele

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.480
from their mother, and a white allele on

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.520
their father's X, so they were carriers.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.760
Cross one with a red-eyed male.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.400
He gives his X, with its red allele,

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.960
to every daughter, and his Y to every

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:16.440
son.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.360
So every daughter has red eyes.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.360
Each son's only X comes from his mother,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.760
so half the sons should have white eyes.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.760
That matches what Morgan saw: no white-eyed daughters,

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.160
and close to half the sons white-eyed.

00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:34.166
People work the same way.

00:01:34.447 --> 00:01:37.886
Haemophilia A, where blood doesn't clot properly, is

00:01:37.967 --> 00:01:40.646
caused by a recessive allele on the X.

00:01:40.927 --> 00:01:43.726
A carrier mother and an unaffected father have

00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:46.366
a one in four chance, for each child,

00:01:46.447 --> 00:01:47.566
of an affected son.

00:01:48.127 --> 00:01:50.206
But for each son, the chance is one

00:01:50.287 --> 00:01:50.926
in two.

00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:55.166
So read the question: all children, or sons?

00:01:55.567 --> 00:01:57.726
No daughter of theirs is affected, because their

00:01:57.807 --> 00:01:59.966
father's X carries the normal allele.

00:02:00.447 --> 00:02:02.446
That's why conditions like this are far more

00:02:02.527 --> 00:02:05.566
common in males: a male needs one copy,

00:02:05.607 --> 00:02:07.246
but a female needs two.

00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:11.426
In a pedigree, X-linked recessive traits leave a

00:02:11.507 --> 00:02:15.106
pattern: mostly males are affected, the allele passes

00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:18.466
through unaffected carrier mothers, and it never passes

00:02:18.547 --> 00:02:19.666
from father to son.

00:02:20.147 --> 00:02:22.426
But it doesn't only come from mothers.

00:02:22.627 --> 00:02:25.266
An affected father passes his X to every

00:02:25.507 --> 00:02:27.906
daughter, and here the mother gives each a

00:02:27.987 --> 00:02:30.986
normal allele, so all his daughters are carriers.

00:02:31.187 --> 00:02:33.346
And one quick test rules it out: an

00:02:33.427 --> 00:02:36.026
affected daughter with an unaffected father.

00:02:36.227 --> 00:02:38.386
That's the family from the last video, so

00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:40.946
that condition wasn't X-linked recessive.

00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:45.046
So far, one allele has always been fully

00:02:45.127 --> 00:02:48.806
dominant, and two heterozygotes gave 3 to 1.

00:02:48.847 --> 00:02:51.286
But that's not the only possibility.

00:02:51.367 --> 00:02:53.446
The fastest way to tell the patterns apart

00:02:53.607 --> 00:02:56.046
is to read the heterozygote first.

00:02:56.327 --> 00:02:58.566
Does it look like one of the homozygotes,

00:02:58.647 --> 00:03:00.806
does it look in between, or does it

00:03:00.887 --> 00:03:02.246
show both at once?

00:03:03.467 --> 00:03:05.946
Snapdragons show the in-between kind.

00:03:06.427 --> 00:03:08.346
Cross a red one with a white one,

00:03:08.467 --> 00:03:10.306
and every offspring is pink.

00:03:10.587 --> 00:03:12.666
The red allele codes for an enzyme that

00:03:12.787 --> 00:03:13.786
makes red pigment.

00:03:14.187 --> 00:03:16.786
The white allele's enzyme doesn't work, like the

00:03:16.827 --> 00:03:19.146
white snapdragons in the proteins video.

00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:22.026
One working copy makes less pigment, so the

00:03:22.107 --> 00:03:22.906
flowers are pink.

00:03:23.387 --> 00:03:25.346
That's incomplete dominance.

00:03:25.627 --> 00:03:27.786
Cross two pinks, and you get 1 red

00:03:27.867 --> 00:03:29.746
to 2 pink to 1 white.

00:03:30.027 --> 00:03:32.266
Red and white come back, so the alleles

00:03:32.387 --> 00:03:33.386
never blended.

00:03:33.507 --> 00:03:34.586
Only the colour did.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:37.240
Cattle show the other kind.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.960
Cross a red bull with a white cow,

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.720
and the calves are roan.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:43.800
Look closely at a roan coat.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:44.840
It isn't pink.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.800
Each hair is either fully red or fully

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.160
white, mixed together.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:52.840
Both alleles are fully expressed, side by side.

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:54.920
That's co-dominance.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:57.560
Two roans give 1 red to 2 roan

00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:59.960
to 1 white, the same ratio as the

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:03.800
snapdragons, because in both cases the heterozygote looks

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.880
different from both homozygotes.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:10.220
Your ABO blood group adds one more twist.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:13.820
In the population, this gene has three alleles,

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:14.460
not two.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.460
That's multiple alleles.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:19.180
But each person still carries only two of

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:19.500
them.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:23.340
One allele's enzyme puts A antigens, small sugar

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:25.740
markers, on your red blood cells.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:28.140
Another puts B antigens on.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.580
The third, small i, makes an enzyme that

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:33.900
doesn't work, so it adds neither.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:37.660
A and B are co-dominant: with both, you're

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:38.780
type AB.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:42.300
Both are dominant over small i, so type

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:44.940
O means two small i alleles.

00:04:46.167 --> 00:04:47.606
Here's the surprise.

00:04:47.847 --> 00:04:50.006
A type A parent and a type B

00:04:50.087 --> 00:04:52.246
parent can have a child of any blood

00:04:52.327 --> 00:04:53.126
group.

00:04:53.207 --> 00:04:55.726
If each carries a hidden small i, the

00:04:55.767 --> 00:04:59.526
square has four boxes: AB, A, B and

00:04:59.687 --> 00:05:02.406
O, each with a one in four chance.

00:05:03.634 --> 00:05:08.353
To remember the three relationships: incomplete is in-between,

00:05:08.434 --> 00:05:10.513
and co shows both.

00:05:10.594 --> 00:05:13.233
And for sex linkage: Dad gives his X

00:05:13.274 --> 00:05:15.953
to daughters, and his Y to sons.

00:05:17.167 --> 00:05:18.766
Two harder questions now.

00:05:19.327 --> 00:05:22.686
First, red-green colour blindness is caused by a

00:05:22.767 --> 00:05:25.086
recessive allele on the X chromosome.

00:05:25.807 --> 00:05:28.126
A woman with normal vision, whose father was

00:05:28.207 --> 00:05:30.926
colour-blind, has children with a man with normal

00:05:31.007 --> 00:05:31.486
vision.

00:05:32.127 --> 00:05:34.446
What's the probability that their first child is

00:05:34.487 --> 00:05:35.646
a colour-blind son?

00:05:36.447 --> 00:05:38.366
What's the probability that a son of theirs

00:05:38.447 --> 00:05:39.726
is colour-blind?

00:05:39.847 --> 00:05:42.686
And could a daughter of theirs be colour-blind?

00:05:42.807 --> 00:05:43.646
Explain.

00:05:43.967 --> 00:05:45.326
Pause here to answer.

00:05:55.734 --> 00:05:58.293
Her father gave her his only X, with

00:05:58.334 --> 00:06:00.773
the colour-blind allele, so she's a carrier.

00:06:01.414 --> 00:06:03.973
Each son gets his X from her, so

00:06:04.054 --> 00:06:06.133
a son has a one in two chance.

00:06:06.694 --> 00:06:08.933
Among all their children, that's a one in

00:06:09.054 --> 00:06:11.013
four chance of a colour-blind son.

00:06:11.734 --> 00:06:14.533
Every daughter gets her father's normal X, so

00:06:14.614 --> 00:06:16.213
no daughter can be colour-blind.

00:06:16.774 --> 00:06:18.213
If you said one in two for the

00:06:18.294 --> 00:06:21.253
first child, you answered for sons, not all

00:06:21.334 --> 00:06:21.973
children.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:23.760
Second.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.240
A mother with type A blood and a

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:28.800
father with type B blood have a child

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:30.160
with type O blood.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:32.800
Work out the genotypes of all three.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:36.160
Then find the probability that their next child

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:37.920
has type AB blood.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:39.840
Pause here to answer.

00:06:50.267 --> 00:06:53.866
Type O means two small i alleles, so

00:06:53.907 --> 00:06:56.146
the child got one from each parent.

00:06:56.427 --> 00:06:58.186
So the mother is type A with a

00:06:58.267 --> 00:07:00.746
hidden small i, and the father is type

00:07:00.907 --> 00:07:02.586
B with a hidden small i.

00:07:03.227 --> 00:07:06.986
Their square gives AB, A, B and O,

00:07:07.067 --> 00:07:09.226
so the chance of type AB is one

00:07:09.307 --> 00:07:09.946
in four.

00:07:10.907 --> 00:07:12.706
If you said a type A and a

00:07:12.747 --> 00:07:14.986
type B parent can't have a type O

00:07:15.067 --> 00:07:17.946
child, remember that each can carry a hidden

00:07:18.027 --> 00:07:18.986
small i.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:22.040
So when a cross doesn't give 3 to

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:24.960
1, read the heterozygote first.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:27.880
In between means incomplete dominance.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:30.200
Both showing means co-dominance.

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.840
More than two alleles in the population means

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:34.240
multiple alleles.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:37.160
And if sons and daughters show different ratios,

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:39.160
look for a gene on the X.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:42.040
Next, you'll use all of these on unfamiliar

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:43.480
crosses and pedigrees.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:46.140
Here are the key notes.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:48.300
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:49.180
to copy them.
